Axis & Allies Engine — Architectural Ruleset (Cursor Enforcement Version)
(Senior Software Architect Guidelines for Godot 4.4+ / GDScript)

These rules define the non-negotiable architectural constraints for all agentic code generation.
Agents MUST follow these rules unless explicitly instructed otherwise.

1. STRICT DECOUPLING (Component & Signal-Driven Architecture)
1.1 No Hard Dependencies
Scripts MUST NOT use get_node(), $, or absolute scene paths to reach parents, siblings, or distant nodes.

Nodes may only call methods downward into their own children.

1.2 Directional Communication
Nodes MUST NOT call methods upward or sideways.

Upward or cross-system communication MUST use typed signals.

1.3 Typed Signals Only
All signals MUST include explicit type annotations, e.g.:

```gdscript
signal battle_resolved(result: BattleResultResource)
signal unit_selected(unit_id: int)
```

1.4 EventBus for Global Events
Use a lightweight EventBus Autoload for:

- Achievements
- Audio triggers
- Analytics
- Global notifications

DO NOT use EventBus for UI-to-gameplay chatter or local scene logic.

1.5 Narrow Parent Interfaces
Children may assume their parent implements a minimal, documented interface, but never a concrete scene structure.

2. DATABASE ISOLATION (Data/Logic Separation)
2.1 Runtime vs Structural Data
Gameplay nodes (e.g., units, territories, factories) may only store runtime state, such as:

- current_hp
- movement_points
- is_exhausted

They MUST NOT store:

- unit definitions
- map definitions
- rule data
- production costs
- IPC values

2.2 All Static Data Must Be Resources
Use custom Resource scripts for:

- Unit definitions
- Territory definitions
- Map graph
- Combat rules
- Production rules
- National objectives
- Technology rules

2.3 SaveManager Is the Only Disk Writer
Gameplay code MUST NOT call FileAccess, ConfigFile, or any disk API.

All persistence flows through SaveManager.

SaveManager maintains an in-memory dictionary cache and serializes it.

3. DATA INTEGRITY & SECURITY (Anti-Tampering)
3.1 Encryption Required
All persistent data MUST be encrypted using:

FileAccess.open_encrypted_with_pass()
or
encrypted ConfigFile

3.2 Fail-Safe Loading
When loading:

- Validate all types
- Validate ranges
- Validate required fields
- Catch all exceptions
- Fall back to safe defaults
- Log a warning instead of crashing

3.3 Never Trust External Data
All deserialization MUST sanitize inputs before applying them to runtime state.

4. AGENTIC WORKFLOW REQUIREMENTS
4.1 Blueprint Before Code
Before writing ANY code, agents MUST output exactly three bullet points:

- Signals emitted (names + typed payloads)
- Custom Resources used (names + purpose)
- How the component isolates itself from UI

No code may be generated until the blueprint is approved.

4.2 No Architectural Changes Without Permission
Agents MUST NOT:

- invent new patterns
- modify architecture
- introduce new singletons
- change turn-flow logic
- alter resource schemas

Unless explicitly instructed.

4.3 Follow the Turn Engine Contract
All gameplay logic MUST respect:

- deterministic resolution
- phase ordering
- validator rules
- state immutability between phases

5. GODOT-SPECIFIC RULES
5.1 Static Typing Required
All GDScript MUST use:

- typed variables
- typed signals
- typed function signatures
- typed arrays and dictionaries

5.2 No Scene-Tree Assumptions
Agents MUST NOT assume:

- specific parent nodes
- specific UI structure
- specific scene paths

All dependencies must be injected or signaled.

5.3 UI Is Always Passive
UI:

- listens to signals
- displays state
- never owns gameplay logic
- never mutates game state directly

6. OUTPUT FORMAT FOR ALL TASKS
When implementing a feature, agents MUST:

Step 1 — Output the 3-bullet blueprint (see section 4.1)
Step 2 — Wait for approval
Step 3 — Generate clean, statically-typed GDScript

follow all rules above
no hard dependencies
no disk access
no architecture drift
